What:           /sys/bus/usb/devices/.../chip_information
Date:           October 2026
Contact:        OpenSparklink <https://github.com/OpenSparklink/linux/issues>
Description:
                Read-only WS73 firmware-reported model and bus, obtained with
                HCC platform GET_CHIP_TYPE (request 16, Complete 10). Example:
                source=HCC_GET_CHIP_TYPE model=WS73E model_id=2 bus=USB bus_id=0

                SDK model IDs: U=0, S=1, E=2. Unknown models, failed status,
                invalid lengths and a non-USB bus reject bootstrap. No model
                is inferred from VID/PID, a physical port, or a firmware name.
                The original two-second query deadline is not renewed by
                unrelated input. Each query write receives only its remaining
                original USB timeout; expiration prevents subsequent writes
                and notification reads. This query precedes Runtime registration;
                it does not consume DLI command or controller data credits.

                Reading during a bootstrap/recovery transaction returns
                EAGAIN. Missing/invalidated controller metadata returns
                ENODATA. The snapshot is serialized with the per-device phase
                lock; an earlier generation's observation is not published
                after invalidation. The attribute does not grant Ready.

                This is a firmware report, not silicon, board calibration or
                executing-firmware attestation. HCC is unavailable on the
                two-endpoint ROM download interface. It cannot select the
                first cold-boot firmware before that firmware runs. Boot
                deployment must supply explicit matching inputs until a
                separately verified ROM identification mechanism exists.

What:           /sys/bus/usb/devices/.../sle_address_source
Date:           October 2026
Contact:        OpenSparklink <https://github.com/OpenSparklink/linux/issues>
Description:
                Read-only bootstrap observation of the SDK SLE address eFuse
                field, using HCC READ_EFUSE request 11, Complete 5, fixed field
                ID 125 and fixed six-byte length. No arbitrary field, register,
                eFuse write, address configuration or flash access is exposed.

                A completed successful read reports:
                source=HCC_READ_EFUSE id=125 response=1 error=0 status=00000000 length=6 bytes=<12 hex digits>

                Firmware failure retains its status and reported length but
                withholds the output bytes (bytes=unavailable). An unavailable
                response reports response=0, error=<negative errno>,
                status=unknown, length=0 and bytes=unknown. This diagnostic has
                one original two-second deadline including TX, unrelated RX
                and notification handling, before Native registration/DLI.
                Timeout is optional diagnostic unavailability; malformed
                related replies and transport errors fail bootstrap. It does
                not consume DLI command or controller data credit.

                Read observations use the same phase lock and metadata
                publication contract as chip_information: EAGAIN during a
                transaction, ENODATA without valid current metadata. They are
                not inherited from an earlier bootstrap/interface generation.

                Zero bytes are observable data, not proof of an assigned or
                valid identifier. Neither a firmware success status nor this
                attribute establishes silicon identity, address uniqueness,
                the firmware's initialization branch or secure attestation.
                The observation does not change the controller address, grant
                Ready, or authorize any connection or security operation.

What:           /sys/bus/usb/devices/<interface>/hcc_memory_observation
Date:           October 2026
Contact:        OpenSparklink maintainers (https://github.com/OpenSparklink/linux/issues)
Description:
                Experimental, root-readable (0400) WS73 diagnostic. Each read
                explicitly performs one SDK USB class/interface IN request 5,
                value/index zero, for exactly four bytes, with a 250 ms USB
                timeout and no retry. Probe admits interface zero only.

                Output is one line:
                source=SDK_USB_CLASS_5 id=N generation=G raw_le32=V start_ns=S end_ns=E semantics_verified=0

                V is the little-endian response word, not DLI data credit,
                Ready, peer delivery or a recovery result. A four-byte reply,
                including zero, does not verify running firmware semantics or
                prove memory exhaustion. Firmware support and interpretation
                require separate evidence. The observation is not cached or
                used by the driver's transmit, health or recovery policy.

                S/E use the kernel monotonic clock and bracket the synchronous
                helper, including allocation/wait. They are not exact USB
                submission/completion or RF timestamps. id/generation label
                this observation; they do not authorize another operation.

                Busy bootstrap/recovery returns EAGAIN without issuing USB;
                an inactive/unpublished Runtime returns ENODATA; a stopped or
                broken device returns ENODEV. Unsupported transport returns
                EOPNOTSUPP. USB errors propagate, a non-exact response returns
                EREMOTEIO, and a successful reply followed by observed stop,
                failure or identity change returns ESTALE without a value.

                Only process context may issue this query. Existing driver
                attribute removal drains readers before disconnect releases
                their context. Queries are serialized with bootstrap/recovery
                by the per-device phase lock; they never acquire a Host lock.
                Normal operation and existing collectors do not read this
                attribute automatically. A diagnostic failure does not create
                protocol credit, controller failure or a successful recovery.
